  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nA shortwave trough will be moving from Middle Tennessee eastward \nthrough the southern Appalachians this afternoon into tonight. Ahead \nof this feature, widespread showers, with some embedded \nthunderstorms, are ongoing across the Cumberland plateau and the \nTennessee valley. Do not believe there's much of a risk of any \nsevere storms today, as instability is somewhat limited (MLCAPE \nvalues are on the order of about 1,500 J/kg). A couple of stronger \nstorms capable of winds in the 40-50 mph range could occur, but \noverall the threat is limited I think. Additionally, PWATs are quite \nhigh (between 1.8 and 2.0\" across our CWA) so heavy rains and some \nlocalized flooding issues could arise if there is any training of \nconvection. This appears to be a limited threat as well, given \nstorm motions, but worth keeping an eye on for sure.\n\nThe shortwave shifts east of the forecast area by late this evening \nand the overnight hours. We likely keep some lingering showers, \nespecially in the northern mountains thanks to aided lift from \nincreasingly upslope flow, into the morning hours on Sunday. But \notherwise we should see increasingly dry conditions to finish out \nthe weekend.\n\nAfterwards, upper ridging builds back into the region, with little \nto no chance of rain outside of perhaps the East Tennessee \nmountains, and afternoon temperatures rising firmly back into the \nupper 80s to mid 90s by Tue/Wed of this coming week. \n\nWe'll have to wait and see how the latter part of the week shapes \nup. Currently some deterministic guidance bring an upper low from \noff the southeast coastline, westward across northern Florida and \ninto the Gulf. This would weaken the ridge and could lead to much \nbetter rain chances across the area. But suffice it to say the \ndetails would matter greatly, so for now the forecast just shows a \ngradual increase in rain chances towards the end of next week.",
